WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:05.420
After all the hype, Guardians of the Galaxy
Vol. 2 proved itself a worthy successor to

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:06.420
the first film.

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:10.980
With another Awesome Mix Tape blasting and
another round of adventures for Star-Lord

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:16.260
and his gang of unlikely heroes, Vol. 2 offered
up the same mix of action and comedy fans

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:17.440
have come to love.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:21.510
And like the first installment, the newest
Guardians is packed with Easter eggs.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:26.609
Here are all the details only true fans noticed
in Guardians of the Galaxy 2.

00:00:26.609 --> 00:00:28.860
Major spoilers ahead!

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.109
Retro tracker

00:00:30.109 --> 00:00:34.739
One of the very first shots of the movie shows
Star-Lord tracking the Abilisk — the giant

00:00:34.739 --> 00:00:38.170
space octopus — on a very retro-looking
piece of hardware.

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:43.870
It looks about as 21st century as Star-Lord's
Awesome Mix Tapes, and for good reason — that

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:49.180
space alien tracker is literally just a Mattel
Electronics Classic Football game.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:53.820
If you squint during the movie, you can even
see a faded "Mattel Electronics" written across

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.339
the top.

00:00:55.339 --> 00:00:57.030
Glimpse of Eternity

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:01.979
When Ego forces his will onto his son and
Peter's eyes fill with stars, Peter whispers

00:01:01.979 --> 00:01:03.460
one word: "Eternity."

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:08.580
To casual fans, it sounds like he's saying,
basically, "Man, that's a big universe."

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:12.540
But it also seems like a reference to a God-like
being from the comics.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:17.530
In the Marvel Universe, Eternity is a character
who encompasses everything in the universe,

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:20.360
a being with tremendous cosmic powers.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:23.450
Remember how Ego is kind of a god, but with
a little "g"?

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:26.610
Well, Eternity is the big G — one of them,
anyway.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:30.530
In Marvel terms, Star-Lord got himself a glimpse
of God.

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:31.610
Heavy.

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:33.490
Grandmaster surprise

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:38.280
One of the best surprises in the Thor: Ragnarok
trailer was the appearance of Jeff Goldblum

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:39.780
as the Grandmaster.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:44.030
And if you thought you'd have to wait until
November to see him in action, you're in luck.

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:49.290
Goldblum makes a brief, dancing cameo in the
credits of Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2,

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:52.880
which...doesn't serve any purpose that we
can tell, but it's a nice nod to the coming

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:55.360
clash of worlds happening in the MCU.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.229
Galaga tactics

00:01:57.229 --> 00:02:02.570
When the Sovereign drone ships attack the
Guardians, we get even more proof that writer-director

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:07.620
James Gunn is completely in love with the
'80s — the drones attack in two streaming

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:12.010
lines, a pitch-perfect flight plan from the
original Galaga arcade game.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:14.100
Even their weapons sound pretty similar:

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:22.319
And when all the Sovereign pods explode in
the quantum asteroid field except one, the

00:02:22.319 --> 00:02:28.359
pilots gather around the last remaining starfighter
— just like kids in an arcade.

00:02:28.359 --> 00:02:29.450
Planet Hulk

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:33.360
After their encounter with the drones, the
Guardians are forced to make an emergency

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:35.409
landing on an empty planet.

00:02:35.409 --> 00:02:40.760
The place fills up pretty fast, though, as
both Ego and the Ravagers track the Guardians

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.379
to the planet's surface.

00:02:42.379 --> 00:02:46.530
But if Quill and crew had spent a little more
time walking around those woods, they may

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:50.550
have seen that they weren't the first voyagers
to crash-land in that forest.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:55.349
In fact, they might have been able to see
a big, Hulk-sized footprint in the ground.

00:02:55.349 --> 00:02:59.719
That's because Berhert, the planet they landed
on, is apparently the same one where Hulk

00:02:59.719 --> 00:03:03.780
fought the Galaxy Master in Incredible Hulk
#111.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:07.409
There's even a race that lives there — the
Sagittarians.

00:03:07.409 --> 00:03:09.049
Contraxia

00:03:09.049 --> 00:03:11.830
Nobody's making up planet names for Guardians.

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:15.779
Just about every planet that shows up relates
back to one that's already been established

00:03:15.779 --> 00:03:16.930
in Marvel comics.

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:22.279
That includes Contraxia, the planet where
Yondu makes his first appearance in the film.

00:03:22.279 --> 00:03:27.290
The first time Marvel readers saw Contraxia
was in Jack of Hearts #2, and it served as

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:30.129
the setting for Jack's adventures for three
issues.

00:03:30.129 --> 00:03:35.510
The planet showed up again in Marvel Zombies
Supreme Vol 1 #4, which saw Jack of Hearts

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:38.169
returning to fight the undead Squadron Supreme.

00:03:38.169 --> 00:03:42.640
Like in Guardians, the Contraxia of the comics
is cold and bleak, the kind of place where

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.689
you're bound to run into an unsavory character
or two.

00:03:45.689 --> 00:03:47.280
Speaking of which...

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.499
Howard the Duck Vol. 2

00:03:49.499 --> 00:03:51.010
Ahh, Howard.

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:52.519
"That's it.

00:03:52.519 --> 00:03:55.120
No more Mr. Nice Duck."

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:59.829
The star of a Terrible movie, but an awesome
Easter egg in both Guardians movies.

00:03:59.829 --> 00:04:04.999
Howard returns in Guardians Vol. 2, this time
relaxing with a drink on Contraxia.

00:04:04.999 --> 00:04:09.059
The camera slides past him pretty quickly,
so you have to keep an eye out for him.

00:04:09.059 --> 00:04:13.299
Howard the Duck might seem like an odd choice
to stick in Guardians of the Galaxy, but to

00:04:13.299 --> 00:04:16.030
James Gunn, if the Duck fits, it sits.

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:17.530
Gunn told Yahoo!

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:21.070
Movies in March 2017 that he's "a huge Howard
the Duck fan."

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:24.030
Well — a huge fan of Howard the Duck comics,
that is.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:27.580
Like the rest of humanity, Gunn thought the
movie from the '80s sucked.

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:32.060
We'll just have to wait until Vol. 3 to see
where the Duck shows up next.

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:33.340
Original Guardians

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:38.260
Guardians Vol. 2 featured a ton of new faces,
but for those who've kept up with the comics

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:41.420
over the years, most of them weren't actually
new at all.

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:48.100
Sylvester Stallone's character, Stakar Ogord,
first appeared in 1975 in Defenders #27.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:53.790
He becomes Starhawk and joins the Guardians
in the first issue of their 1990 run.

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:59.880
During Yondu's epic, technicolor funeral,
we also get to see Charlie-27, Aleta Ogord,

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:04.500
and Martinex T'Naga, who also showed up on
Contraxia in the movie.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:09.070
These characters were all involved with the
older and original incarnations of the Guardians

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:10.320
of the Galaxy team.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:15.240
The post-credits scenes also gave us a glimpse
of Krugarr, who in the comics becomes Doctor

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:16.560
Strange's apprentice.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:21.130
Either Gunn is just paying tribute to the
old Guardians, or they'll all be back in Vol.

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:22.130
3.

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:24.040
Again, we'll have to wait and see.

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:25.540
Adam Warlock

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:29.670
One of the final Easter eggs in the movie
came during one of the post-credits scenes.

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:34.380
Ayesha, the Sovereign's leader, unveils a
golden cocoon that contains a man she only

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:35.940
refers to as Adam.

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:40.860
Longtime fans of the comics realized pretty
quickly that she was talking about Adam Warlock,

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:43.610
a mainstay of Marvel's cosmic comics.

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:47.910
Like in the movie, Adam was created inside
a cocoon in the comics and grew to become

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:52.720
an incredibly powerful character after taking
possession of one of the Infinity Stones.

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:58.130
What his role will be in the MCU and the upcoming
Infinity War is still uncertain, but it's

00:05:58.130 --> 00:06:00.450
definitely going to be a game-changer.

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:02.330
Stan the delivery man

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:06.890
Playing a game of Spot the Stan is always
fun, and Guardians 2 doesn't disappoint.

00:06:06.890 --> 00:06:11.420
But...the plot thickens: When we see Stan
Lee talking to The Watchers as Rocket and

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:16.360
Yondu are portal-jumping across the galaxy,
he tells them about his job as a FedEx delivery

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:17.360
man.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:20.860
You know, which was totally his job in Captain
America: Civil War.

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:23.920
"Are you Tony Stank?"

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:26.690
"Yes, this is Tony Stank.

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:28.900
You're in the right place."

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:32.720
On one hand, it makes for a meta Easter-egg-within-an-Easter-egg
moment.

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:37.540
On the other hand, it's possible confirmation
of a long-held fan theory that Stan Lee is

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:43.120
a Watcher himself — an alien being that
observes momentous events in the Marvel universe.

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:48.560
Even in light of that evidence, the studio
still isn't explicitly confirming that theory,

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:52.310
but think about it: every one of these movies
covers a major event.

00:06:52.310 --> 00:06:57.220
So by Marvel logic, there's probably a Watcher
somewhere in each of those movies.

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:02.850
Someone who always shows up...and always looks
the same...and has no other reason to be there…

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:04.720
Someone like...Stan Lee.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:06.560
"Stan Lee came back?"

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:08.380
"Stan Lee never left.

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:11.920
And I'm starting to think his mind is no longer
in mint condition."

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:13.530
Thanks for watching!

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:16.430
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:18.680
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

